# Cloudflare Pages headers file.
#
# Masi's web build uses dart2wasm + drift's OPFS (Origin Private File
# System) worker. Both require the page to be "cross-origin isolated", which
# only happens when the top-level document is served with COOP + COEP. See:
# https://web.dev/articles/coop-coep
# https://drift.simonbinder.eu/web/#cross-origin-isolation
#
# `require-corp` is stricter than `credentialless` but this app has no
# cross-origin embeds that need the relaxed mode, so pick the safer default.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW CLOUDFLARE PAGES COMBINES RULES — read this before adding a block.
#
# Cloudflare Pages does NOT resolve a repeated header name by path specificity.
# When more than one rule matches a request, it **appends** every matching
# rule's value into a single comma-joined header. An earlier version of this
# file asserted the opposite ("resolves by path specificity, not file order"),
# and that false assumption shipped two live bugs:
#
#   1. `/*.wasm` and `/canvaskit/*.wasm` BOTH matched /canvaskit/skwasm.wasm,
#      so it was served `Content-Type: application/wasm, application/wasm` —
#      not a valid MIME type. With `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` set (as
#      Pages does), a browser cannot recover from that by sniffing, and
#      `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming()` MIME-checks its response.
#   2. `/*` and `/sqlite3.wasm` both matched, so that asset was served
#      `Cache-Control: no-cache, public, max-age=31536000, immutable` —
#      directly self-contradictory directives.
#
# Note that `*` spans path separators: `/*.wasm` already matches
# `/canvaskit/skwasm.wasm`. A second, "more specific" block for a subdirectory
# is therefore not an override — it is a duplicate.
#
# THE RULE: for any given header name, exactly one block in this file may set
# it for a given path. If you need a different value for a subpath, you cannot
# also set that header on `/*` — restructure instead of layering.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Anti-stale-shell reload fix (#55): Flutter's default cleanup service worker
# (`flutter_service_worker.js`) calls `client.navigate(client.url)` on
# activate for any returning visitor still holding an old caching SW — a
# real, user-visible reload. Without an explicit Cache-Control, Cloudflare's
# default heuristic caching lets a stale `flutter_bootstrap.js`/`index.html`/
# `main.dart.wasm` etc. linger in the browser/edge cache, so a returning
# visitor's shell and app code disagree and this reload-reconciliation dance
# repeats. `no-cache` (NOT `no-store`, and deliberately NOT `immutable` —
# Flutter does not content-hash these filenames per build, so `immutable`
# would pin stale code forever) means "cache it, but always revalidate via
# ETag first": a cheap 304 when nothing changed, always-fresh the moment a
# new build lands.
#
# Cache-Control is set ONCE, on the blanket `/*` block, and nowhere else. Per
# the rule above, a second block could only append to it, never replace it.
# There is deliberately no long-cache exception for the two hand-versioned
# drift/sqlite assets: an exception is not expressible here without dropping
# Cache-Control from `/*`, and dropping it would leave every path this file
# forgot to enumerate on Cloudflare's default heuristic caching — which is
# exactly the #55 stale-shell failure. Revalidating those two costs a
# conditional request that answers 304, and offline reads come from the
# service worker's precache (they are in sw.js's manifest), not from the HTTP
# cache, so the long-cache was buying very little to begin with.
#
# This also means /sw.js needs no block of its own: `/*` already gives it
# `no-cache`, which is mandatory — sw.js is how a new build reaches an
# existing installation. `tool/gen_sw_manifest.dart` stamps a content-derived
# SHELL_VERSION into it at build time, that byte change is what makes the
# browser treat the worker as updated, and its install then precaches the new
# shell. A long-cached sw.js would strand a user on one shell version. Do not
# add a /sw.js block to "make that explicit" — under append semantics it would
# produce `no-cache, no-cache`.

/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Cloudflare Pages needs an explicit content-type for *.wasm — without it some
# edge configs serve wasm as application/octet-stream, which browsers refuse to
# `instantiateStreaming()`. This single block covers every .wasm in the build,
# including everything under /canvaskit/, because `*` spans path separators.
/*.wasm
  Content-Type: application/wasm
